th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/monotone-function.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/monotone-function.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_monotone_function_e5551ed5_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does it mean for $f\\colon X\\to\\mathbb{R}$ to be non-decreasing?","options":["For $x \\lt y$, one has $f(x) \\gt f(y)$","For $x \\lt y$, one has $f(x) = f(y)$ only","For $x \\lt y$, one has $f(x)\\geq f(y)$","For $x \\lt y$, one has $f(x)\\leq f(y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ea2838332ff410d93867bda5","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_monotone_function_e5551ed5_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the difference between increasing and strictly increasing in the given definitions?","options":["Strictly increasing uses $f(x) \\lt f(y)$ instead of $f(x)\\leq f(y)$ when $x \\lt y$","Strictly increasing needs $f'(x) \\gt 0$, while increasing allows $f'(x)=0$","Increasing uses $f(x) \\lt f(y)$; strictly increasing uses $f(x)\\leq f(y)$","Strictly increasing additionally requires $f$ to be continuous"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6202f97a312d8efa33506f92","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_monotone_function_e5551ed5_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which function is strictly increasing on all points of its domain that lie in $(-1,1)$?","options":["$f(x)=-\\dfrac{1}{x}$ for $x\\neq0$","$f(x)=\\cos2x$","$f(x)=\\mathrm{e}^{x^2}$","$f(x)=\\sin\\dfrac{x}{2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ecf9adb6f2b0f1fc82ad0a72","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_monotone_function_e5551ed5_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable and strictly increasing. Which conclusion is guaranteed?","options":["$x\\mapsto f(-x)$ is strictly increasing.","$f$ is unbounded above.","$x\\mapsto-f(-x)$ is strictly increasing.","$f'(x)>0$ for every $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ef314d83cb45c10bb2cd7707","time_limit":40},{"id":"expansion_pack_049_monotone_function_add_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which function is non-increasing on $\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["$f(x)=x$","$f(x)=x^3$","$f(x)=\\mathrm{e}^x$","$f(x)=-x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"901b29a1a983a3bae99837a9","time_limit":20},{"id":"expansion_pack_049_monotone_function_add_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A function satisfies $f(1)=2$ and $f(3)=2$. Can it be strictly increasing on an interval containing $1$ and $3$?","options":["No, strict increase would require $f(1) \\lt f(3)$.","Yes, strict increase only forbids $f(1) \\gt f(3)$.","Yes, if $f$ rises between $1$ and $3$, then falls back.","No, since $f(1)=f(3)$ forces $f$ to be constant on $[1,3]$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"05dc08d39da08308f5622945","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_monotone_function_e5551ed5_008_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"How is the constant function $f(x)=2$ on $\\mathbb{R}$ classified?","options":["Nondecreasing and nonincreasing, but not strictly monotone","Neither nondecreasing nor nonincreasing, since its values never change","Strictly increasing and strictly decreasing","Nondecreasing, but not nonincreasing"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5395da77347d26910f59d82c","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_monotone_function_e5551ed5_011_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The signum function $\\operatorname{sgn}$ is nondecreasing on $\\mathbb{R}$ but not strictly increasing. Why does strict increase fail?","options":["It has a jump discontinuity at $x=0$","It is not differentiable at $x=0$","It gives equal outputs to some inputs $x<y$","It has some inputs $x<y$ with $\\operatorname{sgn}(x)>\\operatorname{sgn}(y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2ea172beaa0c098c1863aff6","time_limit":30},{"id":"expansion_pack_049_monotone_function_add_001_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which property does every strictly increasing function $f\\colon I\\to\\mathbb{R}$ on an interval $I$ have?","options":["It is injective on $I$","It is bounded on $I$","It is continuous on $I$","It is differentiable with $f'>0$ on $I$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"628e21efe64470ae9775ac48","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_monotone_function_e5551ed5_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be nondecreasing, that is, $x<y$ implies $f(x)\\leq f(y)$. Let $a,b\\in\\mathbb{R}$ satisfy $f(a)<f(b)$. Which conclusion is guaranteed?","options":["No order of $a$ and $b$ follows unless $f$ is strict.","$a<b$, because $a\\geq b$ would force $f(a)\\geq f(b)$.","Only $a\\leq b$, since the case $a=b$ is not excluded.","$f$ is strictly increasing on all of $[a,b]$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b8bfaa6a4d0f6c14ca653132","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_monotone_function_e5551ed5_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=(x-1.4)^2$ for $x\\geq1$, and let $a_n=f(n)$ for $n=1,2,3,\\ldots$. Which statement about monotonicity is correct?","options":["$f$ is strictly increasing on $[1,\\infty)$, since $a_1<a_2<a_3<\\cdots$.","$(a_n)$ is not increasing, since $f$ decreases on $[1,1.4]$.","$f$ is increasing on $[1,\\infty)$, since $f(x)<f(x+1)$ for every $x\\geq1$.","$(a_n)$ is strictly increasing, but $f$ is not increasing on $[1,\\infty)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2e5c9be06cca1fa93c20e412","time_limit":45}]}}